cards.back:
A spurious wakeup occurs when a thread waiting on a `condition_variable` is unblocked without an explicit `notify_one()` or `notify_all()` call, or without the condition it's waiting for actually being met. This is allowed by the C++ standard for efficiency. It's handled by always re-checking the condition in a loop, ideally using the predicate overload of `wait()`:
```cpp
std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
cv.wait(lock, []{ return shared_data_ready; });
```

cards.front:
Rewrite the following `std::condition_variable` wait loop using the predicate overload of `wait()` for safer and more concise code:
```cpp
std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
while (!data_ready) {
    cv.wait(lock);
}
// ... process data ...
```

cards.back:
```cpp
std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
cv.wait(lock, [&]{ return data_ready; });
// ... process data ...
```
The predicate overload internally handles the `while` loop, automatically re-checking `data_ready` after each wakeup (including spurious ones) while holding the lock, and only returning when `data_ready` is true.
